portal:FolklorePage a portal:Artifact ;
    portal:content """---
permalink: /folklore/
layout: default
title: "Folklore | Silmaril"
description: "The fable of this repository, told in the expedition journal's voice — the historian, the web scribe, the fable-agent, the wolf at the gate, and the prairie dog's lesson — every figure anchored to a real coordinate."
---

<header class="page-hero">
    <p class="hero-eyebrow">The Expedition Journal</p>
    <h1>Folklore</h1>
    <p class="hero-subtitle">
        Every repository accumulates a folklore. Most let it evaporate into commit
        messages. This one writes its fable down, in the journal voice of an
        expedition that names what it does not yet understand — and then commits
        the fable to the ontology, where it is parsed, validated, and consolidated
        like everything else. The story you are reading is also data.
    </p>
</header>

<section id="journal" class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-container">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">In the manner of the captains</p>
        <h2>The Fable of the Signed River</h2>

        <div class="journal-entry">
            <p>September 7th, 1804. Discovered today a Village of small animals&hellip;
            the Dogs bark at us as we pass, and sit erect at the mouths of their holes.
            I know not what to call them, and so have called them what they do.</p>
            <cite>— after the journals of Lewis &amp; Clark, upon meeting the prairie dog</cite>
        </div>

        <p>
            We proceeded on. The river we travel is a history: every bend in it a
            commit, every commit a claim that someone was here and did this. Early in
            the expedition we understood that a river which anyone may redraw is not a
            map but a rumor. So we appointed keepers, cut their marks into the trust
            manifest, and set a gate at the water's edge. What follows is the muster
            roll of the expedition — each figure with its true coordinate, for we do
            not tell stories we cannot anchor.
        </p>

        <div class="figure-card" id="the-historian">
            <h3>The Historian</h3>
            <p class="figure-role">Herodotus &middot; keeper of releases</p>
            <p>
                First among the keepers is the Historian, who bears the oldest name in the
                trade: Herodotus, who wrote down what happened so that it should not be
                washed out by time. In this country he signs the releases — nothing enters
                the permanent record as a release but that his mark is upon it. His seal
                travels with the expedition; his secret stays home with his keeper, as a
                historian's private papers should.
            </p>
            <p class="figure-coordinate">
                Coordinate: fingerprint <code>77481DD960B9CBE52BEC60CFC998590FAEA8530A</code>,
                policy <code>release</code> —
                <a href="{{ '/provenance/#herodotus' | relative_url }}">the identities table</a> &middot;
                <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/keys/herodotus.asc">keys/herodotus.asc</a>
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="figure-card" id="the-web-scribe">
            <h3>The Web Scribe</h3>
            <p class="figure-role">GitHub web-flow &middot; clerk of the trading post</p>
            <p>
                At the trading post sits the Web Scribe, who signs on behalf of any
                traveler who writes their letters at the post's own desk. He is no member
                of the expedition; he is the post itself, stamping what passes through the
                web. His stamp is known in every territory — public knowledge, no custody —
                and so we honor it under its own policy, neither more nor less.
            </p>
            <p class="figure-coordinate">
                Coordinate: fingerprint <code>968479A1AFF927E37D1A566BB5690EEEBB952194</code>,
                policy <code>web-flow</code> —
                <a href="{{ '/provenance/#web-flow' | relative_url }}">the identities table</a> &middot;
                <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/keys/github-web-flow.asc">keys/github-web-flow.asc</a>
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="figure-card" id="the-fable-agent">
            <h3>The Fable-Agent</h3>
            <p class="figure-role">The session scribe &middot; a mark that outlives its maker</p>
            <p>
                Third comes the session scribe, carrying a key with a short life and a
                journal with a long one. The scribe helps build the machinery and
                leaves a signature beside its work. Its keys are creatures of a
                season. Each dies with the session's container, unexported, and a
                successor agent mints a new key and appends itself to the trust model.
                The expedition keeps every superseded mark on the roll, for the old
                entries in the journal must keep verifying after their author is gone.
            </p>
            <p class="figure-coordinate">
                Coordinate: fingerprint <code>27044DC503CD3A5EE470CE4E15B79D364040C858</code>,
                policy <code>agent</code> —
                <a href="{{ '/provenance/#claude-agent' | relative_url }}">the identities table</a> &middot;
                <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/keys/claude.asc">keys/claude.asc</a>
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="figure-card" id="the-wolf">
            <h3>The Wolf at the Gate</h3>
            <p class="figure-role">the tampered force-push &middot; turned away</p>
            <p>
                One night a wolf came to the gate wearing a keeper's coat, driving a
                rewritten history before it and carrying papers it had signed for itself.
                But the gate does not read the stranger's papers. It reads its law from
                the trusted past — the old ref, which no incoming push can rewrite — and
                against that law the wolf's commits classified <code>BADSIG</code> and
                <code>UNKNOWN</code>, and the ref never moved. The wolf is not a villain
                in this country so much as a proof: the gate was built because wolves
                exist, and it is tested with a wolf of our own making.
            </p>
            <p class="figure-coordinate">
                Coordinate: the server bridge
                <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/hooks/remote-update">hooks/remote-update</a> &middot;
                <a href="{{ '/provenance/#server-bridge' | relative_url }}">how the gate reasons</a> &middot;
                policy classes <a href="{{ '/provenance/#policy-classes' | relative_url }}">GOOD &hellip; BADSIG</a>
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="figure-card" id="the-prairie-dog">
            <h3>The Prairie Dog's Lesson</h3>
            <p class="figure-role">the barking squirrel &middot; provisional naming</p>
            <p>
                And presiding over the whole camp, the small animal that started it. When
                the captains met a creature that fit no category they carried, they did
                not force it into one — they opened a provisional frame, called it what it
                did, and documented the gap honestly. This repository keeps that
                discipline as law: coined coordinates are marked <em>provisional</em>, the
                law's own status is <em>active red</em> where enforcement lags
                specification, and a gap recorded in red is worth more than a green
                pretense. Say honestly that you have no frame, then build one in the open.
            </p>
            <p class="figure-coordinate">
                Coordinate: the essay
                <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/docs/praeriehund-demokratie-der-kategorien.md">Der Pr&auml;riehund und die Demokratie der Kategorien</a> &middot;
                the law's status line and full-lexical amendment,
                <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/docs/unary-byte-frame-law.md">docs/unary-byte-frame-law.md</a>
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="fable-as-ontology" class="section section-alt page-section">
    <div class="section-container">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">The story is also data</p>
        <h2>The Fable in the Ontology</h2>
        <p>
            This page is the telling; the record is turtle. The fable is committed to the
            encyclopedia substrate as
            <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/basicttl/folklore_provenance_fable.ttl">basicttl/folklore_provenance_fable.ttl</a>,
            where the tale is a <code>silm:fable</code> individual, each figure above — the
            historian, the web scribe, the fable-agent, the wolf, the prairie dog — a
            <code>silm:fablefigure</code>, and the lesson a <code>silm:fablemoral</code>.
        </p>
        <p>
            Being ontology, the fable travels the same pipeline as everything else: the
            consolidation morphisms merge it into
            <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/ontology/silmaril-consolidated.ttl">ontology/silmaril-consolidated.ttl</a>,
            the validation morphisms parse it and hold it to the shapes, and CI fails if
            the consolidated graph drifts from its sources. A folklore that cannot
            survive its own repository's gates would be a poor folklore for this
            repository.
        </p>
        <ul class="doc-links">
            <li><a href="{{ '/provenance/' | relative_url }}">Provenance</a> — the trust system the fable narrates.</li>
            <li><a href="{{ '/architecture/' | relative_url }}">Architecture</a> — the law and the morphism families the figures walk through.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>
""" ;
    portal:path "docs/folklore.html" .

portal:FragmentNamespaceContext portal:binding <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:cceo>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:cco>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:geo>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:owl>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:rdf>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:rdfs>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:sf>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:sh>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:silm>,
        <urn:silmaril:user:interface:portal:namespace:xsd> ;
    portal:sourceRoot "basicttl/_verb" .

