    portal:path "docs/index.html" .

portal:Page a owl:Class .

portal:ProvenancePage a portal:Artifact ;
    portal:content """---
permalink: /provenance/
layout: default
title: "Provenance | Silmaril"
description: "The Silmaril trust system: three signing identities, a typed verification pipeline, six classification verdicts, and server-side bridges that reject tampered history."
---

<header class="page-hero">
    <p class="hero-eyebrow">Commit Provenance</p>
    <h1>The Trust System</h1>
    <p class="hero-subtitle">
        Every commit in this repository is classified against a trust model that
        lives in the ontology, is rendered into a generated manifest, and is
        enforced by a typed byte-frame pipeline — on your machine, in CI, and at
        the server gate. Nothing is taken on faith; everything is a coordinate.
    </p>
</header>

<section id="identities" class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-container">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">Who signs</p>
        <h2>The Three Identities</h2>
        <p>
            Trusted signing keys are declared as individuals in
            <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/basicttl/commit_signing_trust.ttl">basicttl/commit_signing_trust.ttl</a>
            — the authoritative trust model — committed as armored public keys under
            <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/tree/master/keys">keys/</a>,
            and rendered into the generated
            <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/keys/trust-manifest.txt">trust manifest</a>
            that the verifier consumes. CI fails if the manifest drifts from the ontology.
        </p>

        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="doc-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Identity</th>
                        <th>Policy</th>
                        <th>Key</th>
                        <th>Fingerprint</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr id="herodotus">
                        <td><strong>Herodotus</strong> &lt;herodotus@silmaril.internal&gt;<br>
                            the openclaw agent — release custodian; the secret half lives with the agent's owner</td>
                        <td><code>release</code></td>
                        <td>ed25519<br><code>herodotus.asc</code></td>
                        <td><code>77481DD960B9CBE52BEC60CFC998590FAEA8530A</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr id="web-flow">
                        <td><strong>GitHub</strong> &lt;noreply@github.com&gt;<br>
                            the web-flow service key — signs commits made through the GitHub web UI; public knowledge, no custody</td>
                        <td><code>web-flow</code></td>
                        <td>rsa4096<br><code>github-web-flow.asc</code></td>
                        <td><code>968479A1AFF927E37D1A566BB5690EEEBB952194</code></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr id="claude-agent">
                        <td><strong>Claude</strong> &lt;claude@silmaril.internal&gt;<br>
                            the existing agent signing identity. Its secret halves are session-ephemeral: they die with the agent's
                            container and are never exported. A future session mints a successor
                            <code>agent</code> key and appends it to the trust model</td>
                        <td><code>agent</code></td>
                        <td>ed25519<br><code>claude.asc</code></td>
                        <td><code>27044DC503CD3A5EE470CE4E15B79D364040C858</code></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="pipeline-note">
            Superseded keys stay listed — historical commits must keep verifying. A listed
            fingerprint whose key file is absent is <em>attested</em>: acknowledged, but
            cryptographically unverifiable until the key lands.
        </p>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="verification" class="section section-alt page-section">
    <div class="section-container">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">How verification works</p>
        <h2>The Typed Pipeline</h2>
        <p>
            Verification is not a shell script — it is a family of unary byte-frame
            morphisms under <code>scripts/python/pylib</code>, each one process reading
            bytes and writing bytes, composed by <code>make</code>
            (see <a href="{{ '/architecture/' | relative_url }}">Architecture</a>). The chain:
        </p>
        <ul class="doc-links">
            <li><strong>Trust extraction.</strong> Fingerprint, policy class, key file, and identity are
                extracted from the ontology and rendered into <code>keys/trust-manifest.txt</code>.
                A check gate fails on any drift between the committed manifest and a fresh render.</li>
            <li><strong>Ephemeral keyring.</strong> The committed public keys are imported into a
                temporary <code>GNUPGHOME</code> created for the run — your personal GnuPG keyring
                is never read or written.</li>
            <li><strong>Observation.</strong> Each commit's headers and its
                <code>gpg</code> verification output are captured as byte documents.</li>
            <li><strong>Classification.</strong> Per-commit records are merged and classified into
                exactly one of the six classification verdicts below, keyed by signature type and by the
                signer's fingerprint against the manifest.</li>
            <li><strong>Policy.</strong> A policy morphism reads the classified stream and decides
                the exit status. The classified report is always emitted either way.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3 id="classification-verdicts">Classification verdicts</h3>
        <div class="table-scroll">
            <table class="doc-table">
                <thead>
                    <tr>
                        <th>Class</th>
                        <th>Meaning</th>
                        <th>Default policy</th>
                        <th>Strict policy</th>
                    </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>GOOD</code></td>
                        <td>Valid OpenPGP signature by a fingerprint in the trust manifest</td>
                        <td><span class="token-good">pass</span></td>
                        <td><span class="token-good">pass</span></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>ATTESTED</code></td>
                        <td>Signed by a listed fingerprint whose key file is absent — acknowledged, not verifiable</td>
                        <td><span class="token-warn">pass, acknowledged</span></td>
                        <td><span class="token-fail">fail</span></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>SSH</code></td>
                        <td>SSH-signed commit — outside the OpenPGP trust chain</td>
                        <td><span class="token-warn">pass, warned</span></td>
                        <td><span class="token-fail">fail</span></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>UNSIGNED</code></td>
                        <td>No signature at all</td>
                        <td><span class="token-warn">pass, warned</span></td>
                        <td><span class="token-fail">fail</span></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>UNKNOWN</code></td>
                        <td>Signed by a key outside the trust manifest</td>
                        <td><span class="token-fail">fail</span></td>
                        <td><span class="token-fail">fail</span></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>BADSIG</code></td>
                        <td>Signature present but cryptographically invalid — tampered content or forged header</td>
                        <td><span class="token-fail">fail</span></td>
                        <td><span class="token-fail">fail</span></td>
                    </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>
        <p class="pipeline-note">
            Default hard-fails <code>BADSIG</code> and <code>UNKNOWN</code>; strict accepts
            <code>GOOD</code> only; the report target classifies without gating. Run the report against the
            exact revision being reviewed; a profile or public key alone does not prove a commit signature.
        </p>

        <h3 id="quickstart">Quickstart</h3>
        <pre class="command-block">export SILMARIL_PYTHON=$(command -v python3)
make -C scripts/python/pylib morphism-provenance-commit-policy-default   # verify history
make -C scripts/python/pylib morphism-provenance-commit-policy-report    # classify only
make -C scripts/python/pylib morphism-provenance-commit-policy-strict    # GOOD-only gate
make -C scripts/python/pylib morphism-provenance-trust-manifest-check    # manifest &lt;- trust model
bash scripts/install-git-hook.sh                                         # bridge hooks</pre>
    </div>
</section>

<section id="server-bridge" class="section page-section">
    <div class="section-container">
        <p class="section-eyebrow">The wolf at the gate</p>
        <h2>Server-Side Bridges</h2>
        <p>
            Git hooks cannot be lawful internal callables — their protocol is
            multi-argument with a stdin stream — so they are <em>registered external
            bridges</em>: short scripts in
            <a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/tree/master/hooks">hooks/</a>
            that carry their typed findings in a header comment and delegate all policy
            to the typed tree.
        </p>
        <ul class="doc-links">
            <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/hooks/remote-update">remote-update</a></strong>
                (server side) — the decisive one. The typed tree and trust material are extracted
                from a <em>trusted</em> revision: the old ref value, which the incoming push cannot
                rewrite — never from the pushed tip. A force-push carrying a tampered commit is
                verified against the policy of the history it is trying to replace, classifies
                <code>BADSIG</code> or <code>UNKNOWN</code>, and the ref never moves. A push cannot
                smuggle in its own policy.</li>
            <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/hooks/pre-push">pre-push</a></strong>
                (client side) — every outgoing revision range runs the default commit policy before
                it leaves your machine.</li>
            <li><strong><a href="https://github.com/groenewt/silmaril/blob/master/hooks/pre-commit">pre-commit</a></strong>
                — blocks staged shell syntax errors and staged armored <em>secret</em> key material;
                warns when your signing configuration would classify <code>UNSIGNED</code> or
                <code>SSH</code> (set <code>git config silmaril.requiresign true</code> to upgrade
                warnings to failures).</li>
            <li><strong>CI</strong> — the same make targets run as workflow bridges on pushes to
                <code>master</code>/<code>main</code> and on pull requests: commit policy,
                trust-manifest drift gate, ontology consolidation sync gate, semantic validation gates.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Sources of truth</h3>
        <ul class="doc-links">
            <li>README.md — Commit Signing &amp; Verification (embedded below) — the top-level contract.</li>
            <li>keys/README.md (embedded below) — custody model, key rotation, and regeneration procedure.</li>
            <li><a href="{{ '/folklore/' | relative_url }}">Folklore</a> — the same system, told as the fable it also is.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</section>

{% include documentation.html source="README.md" %}

{% include documentation.html source="keys/README.md" %}

